 right fifty dollars that isn't really much huh there are no sales so how much is it worth

10
00:05:12,610 --> 00:05:14,610
 Yes, well, I suppose you're right.

11
00:05:15,300 --> 00:05:15,730
 Yep.

12
00:05:18,250 --> 00:05:21,700
 I suppose you've got the pan that you'll need.

13
00:05:21,950 --> 00:05:23,300
 No, I don't.

14
00:05:23,960 --> 00:05:25,680
 Here you are, and that'll be $50.

15
00:05:27,120 --> 00:05:30,550
 It'll be wiped out.

16
00:05:30,590 --> 00:05:32,570
 Too bad, but this is the last pan.

17
00:05:43,170 --> 00:05:51,940
 Fever spread all the way to the East Coast.

18
00:05:52,060 --> 00:05:54,360
 Everybody wanted to make a fortune in the gold fields.

19
00:05:54,460 --> 00:05:57,020
 Rich, poor, clerks, priests, they all headed west,

20
00:05:57,400 --> 00:06:00,900
 leaving jobs and family, chasing a golden dream.

21
00:06:00,900 --> 00:06:05,510
 I don't want to go so far away.

22
00:06:05,610 --> 00:06:07,330
 We have a nice life right here.

23
00:06:07,470 --> 00:06:08,670
 Manhattan, why leave?

24
00:06:09,070 --> 00:06:12,470
 I'm going because no man's given an opportunity to get rich twice.

25
00:06:12,570 --> 00:06:14,550
 I'm going to build you our dream home with gold.

26
00:06:14,650 --> 00:06:16,390
 You'll be able to satisfy your every dream.

27
00:06:18,280 --> 00:06:20,340
 You deserve all the good things of life.

28
00:06:22,960 --> 00:06:31,930
 Be sure to unpack all those shoes, Jack, and put them on display.

29
00:06:32,170 --> 00:06:33,590
 Oh, I'm sorry, sir. No time.

30
00:06:33,690 --> 00:06:36,290
 I'm going right now to California to find gold.

31
00:06:36,740 --> 00:06:39,020
 You're my best salesman. How can I let you go?

32
00:06:39,400 --> 00:06:43,660
 Well, I understand. The truth is, sometimes I dream of going out to California to find gold.

33
00:06:43,740 --> 00:06:44,980
 Come on, we only live once.

34
00:06:45,160 --> 00:06:48,670
 I'm going with you, Jack, and my wife will run the store while I'm going.

35
00:06:48,670 --> 00:06:59,420
 Read 'em and weep.

36
00:06:59,420 --> 00:07:03,550
 You fellas here, there's gold in California. Go get it!

37
00:07:03,550 --> 00:07:24,350
 I'm a gold rush too. Well, it wouldn't be the same without me.

38
00:07:24,350 --> 00:07:32,250
 With my boat, you're guaranteed a pleasant trip and you will be in California in three months.

39
00:07:32,250 --> 00:07:33,690
 More like three times three months.

40
00:07:33,690 --> 00:07:34,570
 And you want how much?

41
00:07:34,570 --> 00:07:37,610
 Only $600, meals not included naturally.

42
00:07:37,610 --> 00:07:44,800
 I'll take you to Panama in my clipper, just $500. You walk a little way in no time on the Pacific.

43
00:07:44,800 --> 00:07:49,540
 You take my second clipper and for $500 all the way to California...

44
00:07:49,540 --> 00:07:55,740
 Here's what I'll do. In a real wagon, you can trace the paths of the pioneers at a modest price. $600 cash.

45
00:07:57,010 --> 00:07:59,540
 Well, what do we do? Do we take his wagon?

46
00:08:00,700 --> 00:08:04,220
 Mr. Williams, I'm going to California and look for gold.

47
00:08:04,440 --> 00:08:09,850
 When I'm rich, I'll build a nice house, buy livestock, and if you give your consent, marry your daughter, Sarah.

48
00:08:11,040 --> 00:08:14,200
 No point going away. No, not for that. You don't need gold.

49
00:08:14,340 --> 00:08:18,000
 Ah, thanks, but I want to try my luck. I'm young, and it's now or never.

50
00:08:18,560 --> 00:08:22,500
 Well, then take my best horse for the journey, and good luck to you. I hope you strike it rich.

51
00:08:45,230 --> 00:09:05,410
 I just got here. I'm from Oregon Way.

52
00:09:05,830 --> 00:09:08,420
 Hello. It was Fred. I'm from Manhattan, myself.

53
00:09:08,760 --> 00:09:11,890
 I believe that my claim is right besides your claim.

54
00:09:12,190 --> 00:09:15,570
 Come on. We'll fence it in, like I did for my claim.

55
00:09:15,970 --> 00:09:17,850
 Then I'll show you how to pan for gold.

56
00:09:21,030 --> 00:09:23,820
 It's pretty back-breaking, all right.

57
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:26,300
 But you believe me, it's worth it when you find it.

58
00:09:26,960 --> 00:09:28,280
 There's a good vein here.

59
00:09:28,420 --> 00:09:30,480
 I've already panned about $50 worth.

60
00:09:30,700 --> 00:09:32,980
 Two or three good months, I'll be returning.

61
00:09:33,220 --> 00:09:33,840
 Go back home.

62
00:09:34,200 --> 00:09:35,500
 I suppose you've got plans?

63
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:39,700
 Well, I'm not married yet, but I'm engaged to a girl named Sarah.

64
00:09:39,700 --> 00:09:51,420
 I see. You're a new neighbor. I don't suppose you'd care to relax and play a little poker. What do you say, huh?

65
00:09:51,420 --> 00:09:56,390
 Not playing. I might lose everything in one game.

66
00:09:56,390 --> 00:10:01,120
 Me? I'm going to have a look around. Maybe I might be lucky.

67
00:10:01,120 --> 00:10:26,010
 Some news! Show me a new invention. You pan go quicker.

68
00:10:26,010 --> 00:11:33,190
 It's a steak this big. Say, let's go!

69
00:11:33,190 --> 00:11:50,880
 Folks coming in from all over? It's not so surprising people filling up the whole town, huh?

70
00:11:50,880 --> 00:11:56,860
 Look, a hotel, a saloon, a restaurant, gambling hall, food shops, jailhouse, more restaurants.

71
00:11:57,430 --> 00:12:00,150
 That place was there before, but it's much bigger now.

72
00:12:02,540 --> 00:12:03,380
 Specter's paradise.

73
00:12:04,890 --> 00:12:09,440
 Mine's the best liquor store in San Francisco, and I got the finest whiskey in the West.

74
00:12:10,230 --> 00:12:12,780
 The tenderest steaks in all of California.

75
00:12:13,070 --> 00:12:16,300
 Two pounds apiece, only six dollars, cheap at half the price.

76
00:12:18,790 --> 00:12:25,860
 Then I soak my bread first, and then I take a good whiff of your cooking steaks, that's all.

77
00:12:26,120 --> 00:12:28,320
 You're in no right to smell my steaks for free.

78
00:12:28,420 --> 00:12:29,700
 Pay me half a dollar right now.

79
00:12:29,700 --> 00:12:40,410
 Ha! Meld your steaks and you heard my money clink. The way I look at it, we're square.

80
00:12:41,280 --> 00:12:43,570
 He's right, you're square. Now leave him alone.

81
00:12:44,140 --> 00:12:46,450
 Hey, the old guy's pretty smart, isn't he?

82
00:12:46,900 --> 00:12:49,800
 Get our hot bat there. Sounds good to me.

83
00:12:59,170 --> 00:13:10,580
 Steaks only a pound each, or real ones are three pounds.

84
00:13:11,010 --> 00:13:12,510
 We want the real ones.

85
00:13:13,510 --> 00:13:13,940
 Isky?

86
00:13:14,410 --> 00:13:15,310
 No water.

87
00:13:15,800 --> 00:13:18,680
 Water's good to wash with, but not too often.

88
00:13:20,300 --> 00:13:23,330
 That's what I call a pretty woman.

89
00:13:23,330 --> 00:13:28,860
 But remember, it was in 1849 now, and out of a total population in San Francisco,

90
00:13:29,140 --> 00:13:31,320
 There were only 15 women present.

91
00:13:33,110 --> 00:13:34,860
 That's enough looking at her.

92
00:13:34,920 --> 00:13:37,220
 Now look at what I want and when I want.

93
00:13:37,340 --> 00:13:40,780
 Listen, who are you to tell me what I could look at it?

94
00:13:40,800 --> 00:13:42,980
 Easy to see that she's going to cause trouble, that one there.

95
00:13:54,890 --> 00:13:58,520
 Self-defense.

96
00:14:03,850 --> 00:14:06,810
 No matter when I hear a pistol shot, there I am ready to help out.

97
00:14:06,890 --> 00:14:09,070
 Do you work the funeral at $30 or $60?

98
00:14:09,670 --> 00:14:11,690
 Oh no, the $30 special will do.

99
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:13,990
 To carry him, I will need your help.

100
00:14:14,160 --> 00:14:15,830
 Got the wrong body over there.

101
00:14:15,910 --> 00:14:16,890
 This is the one you want.

102
00:14:16,890 --> 00:14:36,930
 Take a good look around.

103
00:14:37,230 --> 00:14:38,160
 It's a great time.

104
00:14:39,070 --> 00:14:40,490
 Cities are mushrooming all over.

105
00:14:41,030 --> 00:14:44,870
 They're building everywhere. 25 million Americans won't be long.

106
00:14:45,920 --> 00:14:48,980
 25 million? Maybe you're exaggerating some.

107
00:14:49,340 --> 00:14:51,440
 25 million.

108
00:15:12,130 --> 00:15:14,860
 3 to 1.

109
00:15:15,680 --> 00:15:20,220
 Betsy wins, I win. But if it's Calaveras that wins, you lose.

110
00:15:20,490 --> 00:15:20,920
 Is it a bet?

111
00:15:21,020 --> 00:15:21,760
 Yeah, you bet.

112
00:15:29,150 --> 00:15:44,410
 You're still young, and like every youngster, it takes time to settle down. I'm sure you all agree.

113
00:15:48,350 --> 00:15:48,690
 Win them.

114
00:15:49,970 --> 00:15:52,130
 If there's a letter for you, it'll cost you $1.

115
00:15:58,590 --> 00:16:09,630
 No wondering when I'm going to be coming back home.

116
00:16:09,820 --> 00:16:10,720
 What's your news?

117
00:16:10,760 --> 00:16:14,960
 It's a letter from Sarah. She's beginning to miss seeing me.

118
00:16:15,280 --> 00:16:16,800
 Anyway, I'm beginning to miss her.

119
00:16:17,050 --> 00:16:23,790
 I'd like to go back.

120
00:16:37,870 --> 00:16:42,050
 Anything you want.

121
00:16:43,690 --> 00:16:44,610
 Just broke our shovel.

122
00:16:44,960 --> 00:16:45,930
 Where is everything?

123
00:16:46,320 --> 00:16:48,110
 Oh, I mean, all of your stock.

124
00:16:48,510 --> 00:16:49,910
 Well, there you are, one shovel.

125
00:16:50,050 --> 00:16:51,270
 You won't find a better one.

126
00:16:51,370 --> 00:16:52,150
 And how many do you want?

127
00:16:52,210 --> 00:16:53,210
 One, two, three?

128
00:16:53,710 --> 00:16:58,890
 Hey, he's beginning to miss me too much.

129
00:16:59,010 --> 00:17:02,190
 Maybe I'll go back that way.

130
00:17:02,570 --> 00:17:03,820
 Vane's about played out.

131
00:17:03,920 --> 00:17:04,920
 Not much gold lately.

132
00:17:05,410 --> 00:17:08,460
 I'm thinking of Sarah waiting back there, thinking of me.

133
00:17:08,830 --> 00:17:11,300
 I've enough gold now to buy a farm and livestock.

134
00:17:11,710 --> 00:17:14,150
 Then you'll ask Mr. Williams if you can marry his Sarah.

135
00:17:14,470 --> 00:17:16,130
 Well, now look, you're both good fellas.

136
00:17:16,170 --> 00:17:17,950
 So let me give you a little piece of advice.

137
00:17:19,550 --> 00:17:21,170
 An eye on your goal day and night.

138
00:17:21,540 --> 00:17:26,980
 I've got a feeling that there might be a few crooks around who just might be interested.

139
00:17:27,500 --> 00:17:28,960
 Well, partner, so long and good luck.

140
00:17:29,290 --> 00:17:29,600
 So long.

141
00:17:29,740 --> 00:17:29,960
 Thanks.

142
00:17:31,120 --> 00:17:32,520
 Let's check.

143
00:17:32,740 --> 00:17:33,520
 I'll go take a look.

144
00:17:43,180 --> 00:17:50,960
 $10,000 each of us.

145
00:17:51,170 --> 00:17:52,240
 That's about right.

146
00:17:52,480 --> 00:17:53,260
 Pretty, isn't it, Pete?

147
00:17:53,280 --> 00:17:54,060
 It sure is.

148
00:17:54,350 --> 00:17:54,820
 You're right.

149
00:17:55,010 --> 00:17:55,690
 It's time to go.

150
00:17:56,350 --> 00:17:59,430
 Okay, Pete, but we're going to miss each other, I bet.

151
00:17:59,510 --> 00:18:00,150
 That's for sure.

152
00:18:00,150 --> 00:19:24,620
 This movie and art claim is ours, you claim stick.

153
00:19:24,740 --> 00:19:25,180
 Come on.

154
00:19:26,160 --> 00:19:27,380
 You accusing me?

155
00:19:27,650 --> 00:19:28,670
 Are you saying it?

156
00:19:28,710 --> 00:19:30,130
 Was me who moved him?

157
00:19:33,170 --> 00:19:38,880
 Choose me of robbing.

158
00:19:39,430 --> 00:19:39,770
 Go ahead.

159
00:19:58,780 --> 00:20:14,680
 You go, listen, I've got a...

160
00:20:14,680 --> 00:20:24,130
 Always comes up with good ideas.

161
00:20:28,200 --> 00:20:29,920
 Pretty sure it's done.

162
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:31,840
 We can put the plan into action now, right?

163
00:20:32,280 --> 00:20:33,760
 Right. It's now or never.

164
00:20:38,280 --> 00:20:43,840
 Came to bring you a gift.

165
00:20:44,070 --> 00:20:45,490
 A small token of our gratitude.

166
00:20:46,350 --> 00:20:48,030
 Want to thank me, you say?

167
00:20:48,110 --> 00:20:49,710
 What for? I don't understand.

168
00:20:49,830 --> 00:20:50,710
 Why are you thanking me?

169
00:20:50,770 --> 00:20:52,450
 For returning our sacks of gold.

170
00:20:52,790 --> 00:20:54,510
 Yeah, we worked pretty hard to get it.

171
00:20:54,740 --> 00:20:57,660
 Lucky thing, one of your men was so nice, he gave it back.

172
00:20:58,000 --> 00:20:58,810
 Thank you.

173
00:20:58,810 --> 00:21:01,900
 We brought you a roast pig. It'll be mighty tasty.

174
00:21:29,750 --> 00:21:54,720
 It's around a campy two gays. Take them. They're right here.

175
00:21:55,010 --> 00:23:08,820
 Pessengers off to make a fortune, but the other way, they don't have many.

176
00:23:09,580 --> 00:23:13,060
 We'll make you a special price. $200.

177
00:23:13,690 --> 00:23:15,360
 We'll leave tomorrow afternoon.

178
00:23:15,420 --> 00:23:17,020
 Don't worry. I'll be aboard, Captain.

179
00:23:17,310 --> 00:23:19,220
 I don't think we'll be needing this anymore.

180
00:23:19,630 --> 00:23:21,000
 As well, try to sell it.

181
00:23:28,170 --> 00:23:34,880
 Nothing. We'd like to sell some back.

182
00:23:35,020 --> 00:23:37,600
 All first class. We struck it rich with this stuff.

183
00:23:41,200 --> 00:23:45,150
 For the lot.

184
00:23:46,070 --> 00:23:47,410
 Charges over $80.

185
00:23:48,760 --> 00:23:50,310
 This may profit margin.

186
00:23:50,310 --> 00:23:57,520
 It is precisely what I'm needing to pick up gold pieces, but if I'm calculating correct, it's not cheap.

187
00:23:58,090 --> 00:24:01,370
 I might even go so far as to say you are robbing me.

188
00:24:01,890 --> 00:24:03,540
 We'll sell you the same stuff.

189
00:24:03,620 --> 00:24:04,280
 How much?

190
00:24:04,440 --> 00:24:07,680
 Just $20, naturally in cash. What do you say, mister?

191
00:24:08,760 --> 00:24:11,710
 Say yes, you have an honest face.

192
00:24:13,050 --> 00:24:15,900
 Let's spend the 20 on a good meal.

193
00:24:16,020 --> 00:24:18,020
 Two three-pound steaks and an apple pie.

194
00:25:17,050 --> 00:25:18,670
 Subsidials for both of them.

195
00:25:20,550 --> 00:25:24,610
 $60 or $100 unforgettable experience funeral.

196
00:25:24,610 --> 00:25:29,680
 Well, 200 left on the table, well, they will be buried in style.

197
00:25:31,040 --> 00:25:33,040
 Good trip home.

198
00:25:33,120 --> 00:25:33,640
 Bye, Fred.

199
00:25:33,980 --> 00:25:38,470
 Good luck.

200
00:25:46,060 --> 00:25:49,000
 Be shoveling off to each his own.

201
00:25:49,530 --> 00:25:50,400
 Right, partner?

202
00:25:50,910 --> 00:25:54,540
 You've seen for yourselves what it must have been like back in the gold rush.

203
00:25:54,600 --> 00:25:56,080
 Some excitement, some tragedy.

204
00:25:56,240 --> 00:26:01,060
 Life was very hard, and often bandits would come and try to rob a man of many years' work.

205
00:26:01,600 --> 00:26:07,400
 They were all there, farmers and sales clerks and highborn and lowborn and all chasing a dream of riches and gold, you know.

206
00:26:07,660 --> 00:26:10,410
 They created a new lifestyle, new society.

207
00:26:10,410 --> 00:26:15,980
 We can't admire all of them. There were plenty of bad ones. Those bandits, poker cheaters. Come on, admit I'm right.

208
00:26:16,180 --> 00:26:23,000
 Yes, you're right. But what do you expect? As my ancestors said back then, it was a new young country and settling down takes time.

209
00:26:23,280 --> 00:26:31,550
 That's how it was. California would join the Union in 1850 and just ten years later would begin a terrible civil war with brother against brother.

210
00:26:31,770 --> 00:26:35,350
 And it would almost rip the country apart and leave a bitter taste for generations.

